27. See my discussion, with reference to Hutchinson 2001, below in the text of this article."},{"number":28,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.28","text":"28. κεμήλιον is a hapax. As in Voigt: Porro 1996; Liberman 2002. Rodríguez Somolinos 1998:156–57 reads δεκεμήλιον “ que recibe ganado en el sacrificio.” Hutchinson 2001: τονδεκεμήλιον (see his app. crit.). Catenacci 2007, like Liberman 2002.1:62n128, returns to the suggestion of Ludwig Deubner, viz., that κεμήλιον is an adjective formed on the noun κεμάς “young deer.” Those who accept κεμήλιον interpret the sense variously."},{"number":29,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.29","text":"29. I have here translated Lobel’s supplement (σ̣[άωτε); cf. Hutchinson 2001 in his commentary on this line."},{"number":30,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.30","text":"30. This translation combines suggestions from Hutchinson 2001:202 (going back to Lobel) and Andrisano 1994."}],"source_provenance":[{"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_selectors":{"english":".inlineCitation[1]","greek":".inlineCitation[0] pre"},"source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/"}],"source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/","text":"… this the Lesbians\n… a great temenos, far-seen,\nestablished as the common one, and therein altars\nof the blessed gods they put\n\nand they named Zeus God of Suppliants\nand you (they named) the Aeolian, Glorious Goddess,\nMother of all, and the third,\nthis one, they named Kemēlios,\n\nDionysus, Eater of Raw Flesh. Come, with well-disposed\nspirit to our prayer\npay heed, and from these toils\nand from terrible exile rescue us.\n\nThe son of Hyrrhas (i.e., Pittacus) let\nthe Erinys of those men pursue, as once we swore\ncutting …\nnever not any of our companions\n\nbut either dead and clothed in earth\nto lie, at the hands of men who at that time …\nor else having killed them\nto rescue the people from their woes.\n\nOf those men, Pot-Belly took no thought,\nas regards their spirit, but casually\nhaving trod upon the oaths he devours\nour city …\n\nnot according to law …\ngrey …\nwritten (?) …\nMyrsilus … .","translator":"Lowell Edmunds","word_attestation":false}]},{"assignment_fragment":"130b","campbell_identity":{"author":"Alcaeus","edition":"David A. Campbell, Greek Lyric Poetry (1967; reprinted 1976), Macmillan","edition_fragment":"130","kind":"text","language":"grc","quality":"machine_corrected_ocr","raw_sha256":"25229c59dc5ee76abe7a68dfb75bf2c59bbedf3c8f91000579275f2f6ca52e96","source":"campbell_assignment","source_pdf_sha256":"8cbdd94c38c824b7c7eb2920cafac13ac988038cabfe74ddf4f3139e7fa33b9f","source_url":"https://greeklyric.com/sources/campbell-alcaeus/130b.html","text_sha256":"dd1402e22bd6b4f9f7397c92510b66ab5ab8acad66605d07e4f8d1a622ca7a32","work":"Fragments"},"campbell_record_id":"campbell-glp:alcaeus:130b","translation_comparisons":[{"citation":"Fr. 130b V","comparison_id":"alcaeus-translation:edmunds-chs:130b","edition":"Greek text and English translation as printed in Lowell Edmunds, Deixis and Everyday Expressions in Alcaeus frs. 129 V and 130b V","edition_difference_metadata":{"difference_status":"exact_token_diff","exact_token_differences":[{"campbell_token_range":[0,8],"campbell_tokens":["ἄγνοις̣",".",".","ς̣βιότοις̣",".",".","ις","ὁ"],"operation":"replace","other_edition_tokens":["ἀγ