ώω μοῖραν ἔχων ἀγροϊωτίκαν \nἰμέρρων ἀγόρας ἄκουσαι \n4 καρ̣υ̣[ζο]μένας ὦγεσιλαΐδα\n \nκαὶ β̣[ό]λ̣λ̣ας· τὰ πάτηρ καὶ πάτερος πάτηρ \nκα..[.].ηρας ἔχοντες πεδὰ τωνδέων \nτὼν [ἀ]λλαλοκάκων πολίτ̣αν \n8 ἔ.[..ἀ]πὺ τούτων ἀπελήλαμαι \n \nφεύγων ἐσχατίαισ', ὠς δ' Ὀνυμακλέης\nὠ̣θά[ν]ά̣ος ἐοίκησα λυκαιμίαις \nφεύγων τ⌞ον⌟ [π]ό̣λεμον· στάσιν γὰρ \n12 πρὸς κρ.[....].οὐκ ἄμεινον ὀννέλην· \n \n.].[...].[..]. μακάρων ἐς τέμ[ε]νος θέων \nἐοι̣[.....].ε̣[.]αίνας ἐπίβαις χθόνος \nχλι.[.].[.].[.]ν̣ συνόδοισί μ' αὔταις \n16 οἴκημι κ[ά]κων ἔκτος ἔχων πόδας, \n \nὄππαι Λ[εσβί]αδες κριννόμεναι φύαν \nπώλεντ' ἐλκεσίπεπλοι, περὶ δὲ βρέμει \nἄχω θεσπεσία γυναίκων \n20 ἴρα[ς ὀ]λολύγας ἐνιαυσίας\n\n].[´̣].[.].ἀπ̣ὺ πόλλ̣ω̣ν .ότα δὴ θέοι\n      ].[           ´]σ̣κ̣...ν Ὀλ̣ύ̣μ̣πιοι \n                   ]...... \n24 .ν̣α̣[                ]...μ̣εν.","paired_greek_images":[],"paired_greek_transcription_status":"source_html_extraction","quality":"source_html_extraction","reuse_status":"staging_only_noncommercial_unadapted_reuse_required","scope":"whole_poem_other_edition","selection_aligned":false,"source_note_markers":["[ 49 ]","[ 50 ]","[ 51 ]"],"source_notes":[{"number":48,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.48","text":"48. The publication of POxy 3711 (Haslam 1986) permitted the restoration of ὠ̣θά[ν]ά̣ος in v.10 and of φεύγων τ⌞ον⌟ at the beginning of v.11."},{"number":49,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.49","text":"49. See on fr. 130b.10 under the heading “Everyday expressions” in the text of this article."},{"number":50,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.50","text":"50. For the suppositions entailed in this translation see Hutchinson 2001:210–11 and for the meaning of the verb see n. 81 below."},{"number":51,"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/#n.51","text":"51. “To keep one’s foot (feet) out of trouble” is a proverb. Voigt in her register of similia cites Aeschylus Prometheus Bound 263–64; Pindar Pythian 4.289."}],"source_provenance":[{"raw_sha256":"4e0354d528dc388d2b0afe93cf6cd395d0fefb30591c93af68ee813ad276e5af","source_selectors":{"english":".inlineCitation[3]","greek":".inlineCitation[2] pre"},"source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/"}],"source_url":"https://chs.harvard.edu/lowell-edmunds-deixis-and-everyday-expressions-in-alcaeus-frs-129-v-and-130b-v/","text":"Undefiled in life, wretch that I am,\nI live with a rustic’s lot,\nlonging to hear the agora\nannounced, Agesilaïdas,\n\nand the council. That which my father and father’s father\ngrew old in possession of amongst these\nmutually harming citizens,\nfrom this I am driven away,\n\nan exile in the border-land, and like Onomacles\nthe Athenian I settled in, “Wolf-Battle,”\nfleeing the war. For, as for strife,\nagainst … is it not better to be rid (of it)?\n\n… into a precinct of the blessed gods\nI … , having trod on the black earth.\n… myself meetings themselves\nI dwell keeping my feet out of trouble,\n\nwhere the Lesbian women, when they are judged for beauty,\ngo, they of trailing robes, and round about rings\nthe divinely-sounding peal of women’s\nyearly sacred cry.\n\n… from many (troubles) when (will) the gods\n(free me?) …","translator":"Lowell Edmunds","word_attestation":false}]},{"assignment_fragment":"326","campbell_identity":{"author":"Alcaeus","edition":"David A. Campbell, Greek Lyric Poetry (1967; reprinted 1976), Macmillan","edition_fragment":"326","kind":"text","language":"grc","quality":"machine_corrected_ocr","raw_sha256":"a8aec94f417592b5daa1af7e03d68072252e58aedd1160a904e337b64692c158","source":"campbell_assignment","source_pdf_sha256":"8cbdd94c38c824b7c7eb2920cafac13ac988038cabfe74ddf4f3139e7fa33b9f","source_url":"https://greeklyric.com/sources/campbell-alcaeus/326.html","text_sha256":"df602e007e7a1818f14413317de6540b7820d0d9a094d13fb23d9c7f81c9bf86","work":"Fragments"},"campbell_record_id":"campbell-glp:alcaeus:326","translation_comparisons":[{"citation":"Alcaeus, Edmonds fragment 37","comparison_id":"alcaeus-translation:edmonds-1922:326","edition":"Lyra Graeca, vol. I (1922), Loeb Classical Library 28","edition_difference_metadata":{"difference_status":"not_computed_unverified_Greek_transcription","exact_token_differences":null,"extraction_metadata_note":"Edmonds ends before the trailing rudder fragment printed in Campbell. Orthography and editorial presentation also differ."},"evidence_type":"different_edition_translation_comparison","exact_edition_alignment":false,"language":"eng","license":"Public domain in the United States (1922 edition; IA metadata NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT)","license_unrestricted":false,"license_url":null,"line_attestation":false,"model_eligible":false,"paired_greek":null,"paired_greek_images":[{"page_url":"https://archive.org/details/lyragraecabeingr01edmouoft/page/n365/mode/1up","printed_page":"344","scan_leaf":366,"sha256":"1ce1f5233222805605402fa291c6d7b4a0dc353f566a2765a0f09f487794bfc1","url":"https://iiif.archive.org/image/iiif/3/lyragraecabeingr01edmouoft%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_jp2.zip%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_jp2%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_0366.jp2/full/max/0/default.jpg"}],"paired_greek_transcription_status":"withheld_failed_OCR_use_source_scan","quality":"source_scan_OCR_independently_visually_audited","reuse_status":"public_domain_us_edition","scope":"whole_poem_other_edition","selection_aligned":false,"source_note_markers":[],"source_notes":[],"source_provenance":[{"language":"eng","ocr_sha256":"6f99024337b6f13a9e515fe61652ef339f09459c4f9f228d46d625eca0f7510e","source_block_index":3,"source_image":{"page_url":"https://archive.org/details/lyragraecabeingr01edmouoft/page/n366/mode/1up","printed_page":"345","scan_leaf":367,"sha256":"21603047e34016aac6f1244942f49ef22c190548e2f047b5a2af34e886015c80","url":"https://iiif.archive.org/image/iiif/3/lyragraecabeingr01edmouoft%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_jp2.zip%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_jp2%2Flyragraecabeingr01edmouoft_0367.jp2/full/max/0/default.jpg"}}],"source_url":"https://archive.org/details/lyragraecabeingr01edmouoft","text":"I cannot tell the lie of the wind ;¹ one wave rolls\nfrom this quarter, another from that, and we are\ncarried in the midst with the black ship, labouring\nin an exceeding great storm. The water is up to\nthe mast-hole, the sail lets daylight through with\nthe great rents that are in it, and the halyards ² are\nworking loose.³","translator":"J. M. Edmonds","word_attestation":false}]},{"assignment_fragment":"350","campbell_identity":{"author":"Alcaeus","edition":"David A. Campbell, Greek Lyric Poetry (1967; reprinted 1976), Macmillan","edition_fragment":"350","kind":"text","language":"grc","quality":"machine_corrected_ocr","raw_sha256":"2fc0da4a1f959e1544ae3d1e227ad772959175713259c82a2ff93024910e4925","source":"campbell_assignment","source_pdf_sha256":"8cbdd94c38c824b7c7eb2920cafac13ac988038cabfe74ddf4f3139e7fa33b9f","source_url":"https://greeklyric.com/sources/campbell-alcaeus/350.html","text_sha256":"827dc3b0c8c1fd564f0dd9ac97c8752cfd0b74de7fb0303c9400c71be3637838","work":"Fragments"},"campbell_record_id":"campbell-glp:alcaeus:350","translation_comparisons":[{"citation":"Alcaeus, Edmonds fragment 133","comparison_id":"alcaeus-translation:edmonds-1922:350","edition":"Lyra Graeca, vol. I (1922), Loeb Classical Library 28","edition_difference_metadata":{"difference_sta